nd around the Gold Mountain Burn Scar.\n\nSOURCE...Public reported.\n\nIMPACT...Life threatening flash flooding of areas in and around the\nGold Mountain Burn Scar.\n\n* Some locations that will experience flash flooding include...\nHighway 550 from Portland to Ouray and over the Gold Mountain burn\nscar.","ends":"2026-09-17T03:30:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Gunnison, CO; Hinsdale, CO; Ouray, CO","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.73f0b275b56d4aba42806388f0ca716585d67d6f.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.73f0b275b56d4aba42806388f0ca716585d67d6f.001.1"},{"event":"Flood Watch","headline":"Flood Watch issued September 16 at 9:39AM MDT until September 18 at 12:00AM MDT by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"* WHAT...Flooding caused by excessive rainfall continues to be\npossible.\n\n* WHERE...A portion of southwest Colorado, including the following\nareas, Animas River Basin, Four Corners/Upper Dolores River,\nNorthwest San Juan Mountains, San Juan River Basin and Southwest\nSan Juan Mountains.\n\n* WHEN...Through Thursday evening.\n\n* IMPACTS...Excessive runoff may result in flooding of rivers,\ncreeks, streams, and other low-lying and flood-prone locations.\nThis also includes the Gold Mountain, Elk and Ferris burn scars as\nwell as areas of steep, susceptible terrain.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- Recent heavy rainfall in southwest Colorado has led to already\nsaturated ground. In addition, multiple rounds of showers and\nthunderstorms are expected Wednesday afternoon through Thursday\nevening producing periods of heavy rainfall and potential flash\nflooding.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-09-18T00:00:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Northwestern San Juan Mountains; Southwest San Juan Mountains; Four Corners/Upper Dolores River; Animas River Basin; San Juan River Basin","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.d0f7f9a5a0805fda6f1988fda5f919f56cf2d735.002.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2